@page "/candlesticks"

<PageTitle>Candlesticks · RioCharts</PageTitle>

<h1>Candlesticks</h1>
<p class="lead">
    A candlestick shows how a value moved within each period: where it started, how high and low it went, and where it ended. Prices,
    but also measurements: the first, highest, lowest and last temperature per hour or day.
</p>

<Example Title="From readings" Code="@ReadingsCode">
    <Description>
        <p>
            Give <code>CandlestickSeries</code> the raw readings and a <code>Bucket</code>: the readings of each hour (or day, week, …) become
            one candle, in the series color: a rising period is lightly filled, a falling one solid. Three
            weeks of hourly candles are too narrow to see, so they merge into longer periods of the clock and calendar (6 or 12 hours, days,
            depending on the width) that keep the highs and lows; drag to zoom in and the hours come back. Note the thunderstorm on the
            tenth afternoon.
        </p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart TItem="Reading" Items="Outdoor" YAxis="Celsius" Height="280" Zoomable="true">
            <CandlestickSeries Name="Outdoor" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Bucket="TimeBucket.Hour" Format="0.0 °C" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<Example Title="From numbers computed elsewhere" Code="@PrecomputedCode">
    <Description>
        <p>
            With one item per period, pass <code>OpenValue</code>, <code>HighValue</code>, <code>LowValue</code> and <code>CloseValue</code>.
            For prices, <code>DirectionColors="true"</code> colors rising candles green and falling ones red (<code>--rio-positive</code> and
            <code>--rio-negative</code>) and adds the change in percent to the tooltip. It is off by default: red and green read as good
            and bad, and a percentage of, say, a temperature means nothing. Here a line on the same chart shows the average of each
            day's first and last price.
        </p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart TItem="PriceDay" Items="Prices" YAxis="PerMwh" Height="260">
            <CandlestickSeries Name="Price" XValue="p => p.Date" OpenValue="p => p.First" HighValue="p => p.Highest"
                               LowValue="p => p.Lowest" CloseValue="p => p.Last" Format="0.0 €" DirectionColors="true" />
            <LineSeries Name="Midpoint" XValue="p => p.Date" YValue="p => (p.First + p.Last) / 2" Curve="CurveType.Monotone" Format="0.0 €" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<h2>In JSON definitions</h2>
<p>
    <code>"type": "candlestick"</code> (also <code>"ohlc"</code>, <code>"candle"</code>) with a time <code>x</code>, <code>y</code> and a
    <code>timeBucket</code> makes candles from the rows' y values per bucket (a day when not set). With <code>open</code>, <code>high</code>
    and <code>low</code>, each row is a candle and <code>y</code> its close. <code>"directionColors": true</code> colors candles by direction.
</p>

@code {
    private static readonly AxisOptions Celsius = new() { Title = "°C" };
    private static readonly AxisOptions PerMwh = new() { Title = "€/MWh" };

    private const string ReadingsCode = """
        <RioChart TItem="Reading" Items="readings" Zoomable="true">
            <CandlestickSeries Name="Outdoor" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value"
                               Bucket="TimeBucket.Hour" Format="0.0 °C" />
        </RioChart>

        @code {
            record Reading(DateTime Time, double? Value); // every 15 minutes
        }
        """;

    private const string PrecomputedCode = """
        <RioChart TItem="PriceDay" Items="prices">
            <CandlestickSeries Name="Price" XValue="p => p.Date" OpenValue="p => p.First" HighValue="p => p.Highest"
                               LowValue="p => p.Lowest" CloseValue="p => p.Last" Format="0.0 €" DirectionColors="true" />
            <LineSeries Name="Midpoint" XValue="p => p.Date" YValue="p => (p.First + p.Last) / 2" />
        </RioChart>
        """;
}
